28542	{"artifactId":"LLD-1f7ade1a889013da-s1","entry":{"id":"migrated-LLD-1f7ade1a889013da-s1-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:controller","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:31:45.014Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"p2","kind":"closed-union","severity":"HIGH","premise":"DOCGEN_OUTCOME_SCHEMA in src/docgen/schema.ts is the ONLY schema constant in non-test src/ whose ROOT is a oneOf/anyOf/allOf union. This is the premise the entire backwardCompat argument rests on — if a second root-union schema exists and is provider-bound, the guard is a breaking change rather than a latent-case guard.","evidence":"The probe REFUTES the premise. `^\\s*(oneOf|anyOf|allOf):\\s*\\[` returned THREE src matches, not one: src/docgen/schema.ts:73 (`oneOf: [`), src/daemon/tools/builtins/db/index.ts:1846 (`anyOf: [`), and src/workflow/triage/classify.ts:84 (`allOf: [`). Reading each: db/index.ts:1846 is NESTED under `properties.key` inside a tool inputSchema, so it is correctly outside the claim. But src/workflow/triage/classify.ts:84 is a ROOT-LEVEL `allOf` on CLASSIFY_SCHEMA (declared :77), sitting as a sibling of `type: 'object'`, `additionalProperties: false` and `required`, and used as a CONJUNCTION carrying an if/then conditional-required ('A `bugfix` classification MUST carry a magnitude'), not as a union. It is not a test file, and its own doc comment says it is 'Exported so the controller-driven `insrc_triage` tool can hand it to the outer LLM turn' — start.ts:58 does exactly that. So a second non-test root-level union KEYWORD exists in src/, and the LLD's inventory premise is false as written.","action":"This is not merely a miscount — it exposes a design defect in the guard's specification, with a statable failure. `rootUnionKey` as specified returns the keyword for ANY of the three at the root, and the LLD's s5 edge case #4 explicitly requires that a root `type: 'object'` must NOT suppress the guard. Built as written, `rootUnionKey(CLASSIFY_SCHEMA)` returns 'allOf', so the moment anything routes the triage classification turn through `CliProvider.completeStructured` the guard rejects locally a schema that is a legitimate JSON Schema conjunction both vendors almost certainly accept — and the rejection message would tell the caller to 'wrap the union in an object envelope', which is nonsensical advice for an if/then conditional-required. CLASSIFY_SCHEMA does not reach completeStructured TODAY (its only consumers are validateAgainstSchema at mcp/triage-step/phases/classify.ts:23 and the MCP tool response at start.ts:58), so this is latent rather than currently breaking — but it inverts the Story's own stated test: 'if any currently-passing call starts failing, the guard is mis-scoped, not the caller.' Fix the keyword set before build, and correct the inventory premise to say 'the only root-level UNION (oneOf/anyOf)' rather than conflating `allOf` with a union.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"`allOf` is a conjunction, not a union. The empirical grounding behind this whole Story covers the `anyOf`-rooted discriminated-union shape only — the live suite comment at local-agents-structured.live.test.ts:26 says precisely that the '`anyOf`-rooted case is specifically the shape that broke direct' use. Nothing in the gathered evidence establishes that a root `allOf` ALONGSIDE a root `type: 'object'` is rejected by either vendor, and the repo actively relies on that shape. Lumping `allOf` in was an unexamined generalisation from the ISSUE's wording, and it is the one place this LLD repeats the mistake it was written to remove: asserting a vendor behaviour that was never probed.","options":["RECOMMENDED — Narrow the guard to `oneOf` and `anyOf` only; drop `allOf` from the keyword set. Rename the predicate accordingly so the name does not over-promise. This matches the only shape the evidence actually supports, exempts CLASSIFY_SCHEMA by construction rather than by exception, and keeps the s5 edge case #4 rule (root `type` does not suppress) intact for the two genuine union keywords. Record the `allOf` question as an open question rather than answering it unprobed.","Keep all three keywords but reject only when there is NO root `type` — i.e. only when the union IS the schema's whole shape. This exempts CLASSIFY_SCHEMA and still covers a root `allOf`, but it DIRECTLY CONTRADICTS s5 edge case #4 and reintroduces exactly the `'type' in root` early-return that normaliseSchemaForAnthropic (structured-output.ts:343) was deleted for, so it must not be chosen without first settling empirically whether a root `type` changes the vendors' answer.","Keep the guard as written and document that CLASSIFY_SCHEMA can never be routed through CliProvider.completeStructured. Weakest option: it permanently forbids a legitimate, in-use schema shape on the strength of an assumption no probe supports."]}},{"claimId":"p17","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"BOTH `claude --json-schema` and `codex --output-schema` REJECT a schema whose root is a oneOf/anyOf/allOf union, and injecting a root `type: 'object'` does NOT make either accept it. This is the single premise the whole Story rests on and it is NOT verifiable from the source tree — it was established by live probing in a prior session, and the live fixture that documented it was deleted in commit 617734f. Flagging it explicitly: a deterministic probe CANNOT confirm it, and the LLD's own design deliberately avoids re-probing it with a billed call. If this premise is wrong, the guard rejects schemas that would actually work.","evidence":"UNVERIFIABLE from source, as expected for an external contract — and the evidence narrows it further than the premise claims. The only in-repo corroboration is local-agents-structured.live.test.ts:25-26: 'anyOf-rooted discriminated union, nested array-of-objects). The `anyOf`-rooted case is specifically the shape that broke direct' use. That supports the rejection for an `anyOf`-ROOTED union with no root `type`. It says NOTHING about `allOf`, and nothing about whether a root `type: 'object'` alongside a union changes either vendor's answer. The fixture that exercised the shape was deleted in 617734f, so the repo now holds only this comment as its record.","action":"Keep the premise but scope it to what was actually observed. This MED is the root of the p2 HIGH: the LLD generalised one probed shape (`anyOf`-rooted, no root type) into a three-keyword, type-insensitive rule, and that generalisation is what collides with CLASSIFY_SCHEMA. Narrow the stated contract to the `oneOf`/`anyOf`-rooted case, and record the two unprobed extensions — root `allOf`, and root-`type`-present — as explicit open questions. Note the ISSUE itself asked for the guard to be assertable WITHOUT a billed call, which means these two questions cannot be settled inside this Story; that is a reason to narrow the claim, not to assume it.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The Story's accuracy-first posture requires the guard to claim exactly as much as was verified and no more. A narrower guard that under-rejects merely restores today's behaviour for unprobed shapes; an over-broad one rejects working schemas, which is strictly worse and is what p2 demonstrates concretely.","options":["Scope the external contract to '`oneOf`/`anyOf` at the root with no root `type`' — the shape the deleted fixture actually exercised — and list root-`allOf` and root-`type`-present as open questions.","Spend one deliberate, one-off billed probe (outside the hermetic suite) to settle `allOf` and root-`type`-present, then widen or narrow the keyword set on real evidence. Costs a billed call but removes the assumption permanently; the ISSUE forbids a billed call in the GUARD'S TEST, not in a one-time design probe."]}},{"claimId":"p10","kind":"citation","severity":"MED","premise":"validateAgainstSchema is declared at src/agent/providers/structured-output.ts:80, runs ajv locally with no vendor involved, and is the module's most-used export with on the order of 40 call sites across analyze/, workflow/, mcp/ and the providers.","evidence":"The declaration is confirmed verbatim: src/agent/providers/structured-output.ts:80 reads 'export function validateAgainstSchema<T>('. The '~40 call sites' figure is NOT verified — the bare `validateAgainstSchema` grep returned a result set capped at 50 matches, so the probe can only establish 'at least 50 matches across src/ and docs/', not a count of call sites.","action":"Soften the count to a verified form ('the module's most-used export, 50+ matches across src/') or drop the number. Nothing in the design depends on the figure — it only supports the uncontroversial point that validateAgainstSchema is widely used and must keep accepting root unions — so the prescribed change is unaffected.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"An unverified precise-looking number is the kind of detail a later reader will trust without re-deriving. Either verify it or state the bound the evidence supports.","options":["Replace '~40 call sites' with 'widely used across analyze/, workflow/, mcp/ and both providers' — no number.","Re-derive the exact count with an uncapped search at build time and state the real figure."]}},{"claimId":"p21","kind":"ordering","severity":"MED","premise":"The migration ordering is load-bearing: the predicate must be added and unit-proven (steps 2-3) BEFORE it is wired into completeStructured (step 5), and the falsifying mutation must be run and confirmed red (step 6) BEFORE the integration test is accepted. Only step 5 changes runtime behaviour.","evidence":"Not addressed by the gathered evidence. The only read returned was the artifact's own first line, 'docs/standalone/both-cli-providers-reject-top-level-E202610031f7ade1a/S001/LLD.md:1' => '<!-- insrc:artifact LLD-1f7ade1a889013da-s1 -->', which confirms the artifact exists but says nothing about its internal step ordering.","action":"None required. The claim is an internal-consistency statement about the LLD's own migration ordering, which is self-evident on reading the document; a source-tree probe structurally cannot verify it. Recorded as unverifiable rather than asserted as confirmed.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p1","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"normaliseSchemaForAnthropic has ZERO references anywhere in src/ outside its own declaration in structured-output.ts — no production caller and no test caller.","evidence":"Confirmed. The read resolves exactly: src/agent/providers/structured-output.ts:341 => 'export function normaliseSchemaForAnthropic(schema: StructuredSchema): StructuredSchema {'. The bare-name grep returned 23 matches and EVERY one outside that declaration is in docs/ (the ISSUE.md and this LLD itself) — not a single src/ reference other than the declaration, and none in any test. The zero-caller premise holds, so the deletion is clean.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p3","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"DOCGEN_OUTCOME_SCHEMA is never handed to an LLM provider; its only consumer is a direct ajv compile in its own test file, so the vendor root-union constraint has no bearing on it today.","evidence":"Confirmed. src/docgen/__tests__/schema.test.ts:22 reads 'const validate = ajv.compile(DOCGEN_OUTCOME_SCHEMA);' — a direct ajv compile. Of the 26 DOCGEN_OUTCOME_SCHEMA matches, the src/ ones are its declaration and that test; the remainder are docs/ artifacts from the docgen epic. No provider call path consumes it, so the schema is ajv-only and the vendor constraint does not bear on it.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p4","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"CliProvider.completeStructuredOnce is the ONLY code path in the repo by which a StructuredSchema reaches a vendor CLI flag (--json-schema for claude, --output-schema for codex). This premise is what makes a single guard call site provably sufficient for both vendors.","evidence":"Confirmed. Both reads resolve verbatim: cli-provider.ts:193 => \"'--json-schema', JSON.stringify(schema),\" and :210 => \"const args = ['exec', '--output-schema', schemaPath, '--json', ...this.modelArgs(opts)];\". Across all three greps the ONLY src/ occurrences of either vendor flag are in cli-provider.ts, and `completeStructuredOnce` appears in src/ only at :180 (the withTransientRetry delegation) and :183 (its declaration). So the single-chokepoint premise holds and one guard site provably covers both vendors.","action":"none — verified sound. Worth noting for the reader, not as a defect: the p5 evidence shows `completeStructured` also exists on mcp-sampling-provider.ts:210 and ollama.ts:536. Those are correctly out of scope — neither passes a schema to a vendor CLI flag — so the guard is deliberately CliProvider-only, matching a constraint that is specific to the two CLI binaries.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p5","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"CliProvider.completeStructured is declared at src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:175 and delegates to withTransientRetry wrapping completeStructuredOnce — so a guard placed at the top of completeStructured sits OUTSIDE the retry wrapper.","evidence":"Confirmed. cli-provider.ts:175 => 'async completeStructured<T>(' and :183 => 'private async completeStructuredOnce<T>(' both resolve verbatim, and the p4 evidence shows :180 delegating via withTransientRetry. `private async completeStructuredOnce<T>` is unique in src (1 match), so there is exactly one inner method. A guard at the top of completeStructured therefore does sit outside the retry wrapper, as the design requires.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p6","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"withTransientRetry is declared at src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:151 and retries up to 3 attempts with a 2000ms-times-attempt backoff, re-throwing immediately when the error is not classified transient.","evidence":"Confirmed precisely. cli-provider.ts:151 => 'private async withTransientRetry<T>(label: string, op: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {'; :152 => 'const maxAttempts = 3'; :161 => 'const backoffMs = 2000 * attempt'. The 3-attempt / 2s-then-4s backoff figures the LLD cites are exact, so the '< 1500ms' wall-clock bound in the test strategy is a sound discriminator between one throw and three retries.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p7","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"isTransientCliError decides retryability by REGEX STRING MATCH on the error message, and its pattern includes the tokens the LLD requires the guard message to avoid (api error, overloaded, rate limit, too many requests, internal server error, service unavailable, timeout, and the bare status numbers). This is the mechanical justification for placing the guard outside the retry wrapper.","evidence":"Confirmed. cli-provider.ts:409 => 'function isTransientCliError(message: string): boolean {' and :410 is the regex itself: '/connection closed mid-response|api error|overloaded|rate.?limit|too many requests|internal server error|service unavailab...' — matching the vocabulary the LLD requires the guard message to avoid. :160 => 'if (attempt >= maxAttempts || !isTransientCliError(msg)) throw err;' confirms retryability is decided by that string match on the message. The mechanical justification for placing the guard outside the wrapper is sound.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p8","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The LLD states isTransientCliError is currently module-private (not exported), which is why the Story must widen it to an export for the non-retryability assertion.","evidence":"Confirmed. '^function isTransientCliError' matches src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:409 — no `export` keyword — while 'export function isTransientCliError' matches ONLY this LLD's own prose at LLD.md:96. So the symbol is module-private today, and the Story's planned visibility widening is a real, necessary change rather than a restatement of the status quo.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p9","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"CliProviderOpts exposes an optional binPath override at src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:80, which is the mechanism the test strategy relies on to drive the real provider path with an impossible binary and no stubbing.","evidence":"Confirmed, and the test mechanism it underwrites is sound. cli-provider.ts:80 => 'readonly binPath?: string | undefined;' resolves verbatim, and :104 => 'this.binPath = opts.binPath ?? opts.kind;' shows the override is honoured with a fall back to the bare kind name. That is exactly why the test strategy's warning matters: passing the bare 'claude'/'codex' would resolve the user's real installed binary, so an impossible absolute path is required.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p11","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"processSchemaForOpenAIStrict's inner walker rewrites oneOf to anyOf and then recurses THROUGH anyOf/allOf branches rather than rejecting them — in-repo evidence that unions below the root are a supported shape and that the new guard must therefore be root-only.","evidence":"Confirmed. structured-output.ts:261 => \"obj['anyOf'] = obj['oneOf'];\" (the oneOf->anyOf rewrite) and :302 => \"for (const arrKey of ['anyOf', 'allOf'] as const) {\" (recursing THROUGH union branches), with :259 => '// Rewrite oneOf -> anyOf BEFORE recursing into branches.' This is solid in-repo evidence that unions BELOW the root are a supported shape the existing machinery walks into rather than refuses — so the new guard being root-only is correct, and over-rejection via a tree walk would be a real regression.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p12","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"processSchemaForOpenAIStrict has NO production caller — it is referenced only from the gated live test suite and one doc comment in shared/types.ts. The LLD records this as a pre-existing gap it deliberately does not fix.","evidence":"Confirmed. All 9 processSchemaForOpenAIStrict matches are accounted for: the declaration at structured-output.ts:246 and a doc-comment mention at :20; three in the GATED live suite (local-agents-structured.live.test.ts:53, :244, :327); and one doc comment at shared/types.ts:199 ('after `processSchemaForOpenAIStrict` pre-flight.'). Zero production call sites, exactly as the LLD states.","action":"none — verified sound. The LLD correctly flags this as a pre-existing gap it deliberately does NOT fix, which keeps it from being read as a claim that the OpenAI-strict path is wired.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p13","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"notImplementedStructuredOutput is declared at src/agent/providers/structured-output.ts:359 and also has ZERO callers, yet the LLD deliberately LEAVES IT IN PLACE as a stub contract rather than deleting it.","evidence":"Confirmed. structured-output.ts:359 => 'export function notImplementedStructuredOutput(provider: string): never {' resolves verbatim, and of 9 total matches only that one is in src/ — the rest are docs/. Zero callers, as claimed, and the LLD's decision to leave it in place is an explicit judgement rather than an oversight.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p14","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The doc comment block beginning 'Phases B.1-B.5 land per-provider implementations' physically precedes normaliseSchemaForAnthropic's own doc comment rather than the notImplementedStructuredOutput it describes, so the file currently reads as if the normalise helper were the not-implemented stub.","evidence":"Confirmed by the three reads taken together. structured-output.ts:320 => ' * Phases B.1-B.5 land per-provider implementations one at a time. Each' sits BEFORE :328 => \" * `type: 'object'` declaration. TypeBox `Type.Union([Type.Object(...),\" — which is the body of the normalise helper's own comment — and both precede :341, the normalise declaration. So the stub's doc block does indeed sit above the normalise helper rather than above notImplementedStructuredOutput at :359, and the file reads as the LLD describes.","action":"none — verified sound. The LLD's instruction to READ the region after deleting, rather than assume the mis-attachment self-corrects, is the right level of caution given the comment blocks are adjacent.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p15","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"There is NO hermetic (non-live) test file covering src/agent/providers/structured-output.ts — the only suite exercising its schema-shaping half is local-agents-structured.live.test.ts, so the new test file is genuinely the first.","evidence":"Confirmed. \"from '../structured-output.js'\" returns exactly ONE match in src/ — local-agents-structured.live.test.ts:53, the gated live suite — and the deeper-relative form returns zero. So no hermetic test in src/agent/providers/__tests__/ imports structured-output.ts, and the new file genuinely is the module's first non-live test.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p16","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The existing structured-output live suite is gated on INSRC_LIVE_TESTS=1 and additionally on which claude / which codex, and it spawns the real billed binaries — which is why the LLD adds nothing to it.","evidence":"Confirmed. local-agents-structured.live.test.ts:55 => \"const GATE = process.env['INSRC_LIVE_TESTS'] === '1';\" resolves verbatim, and the suite's tests carry 'skip: !GATE || claudeAvailable...' / 'skip: !GATE || codexAvailable...' at :280, :308, :349, :353 — the double gate on the env var AND binary availability the LLD describes. The same GATE idiom appears in 21 src/ files, so it is the repo's established convention for billed suites and the new file's exclusion from it is meaningful.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p18","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"normaliseSchemaForAnthropic's implementation returns the schema UNCHANGED whenever a root `type` key is already present, and otherwise only injects `type: 'object'` — so its algorithm is exactly the one the LLD claims is falsified, and the new guard's requirement that a root `type` must NOT suppress rejection is a direct contradiction of it.","evidence":"Confirmed exactly. structured-output.ts:343 => \"if ('type' in root) {\" is the early return, and :356 => \"return { ...root, type: 'object' };\" is the sole mutation. So the helper's entire algorithm is: leave the schema alone if a root `type` exists, else inject `type: 'object'`. That is precisely the algorithm the LLD describes and precisely the premise its s5 edge case #4 forbids the new guard from inheriting.","action":"none — verified sound. Note this verdict is what makes the p2/p17 findings sharp rather than pedantic: the LLD is right that root-`type`-insensitivity is the correct posture for a genuine union, and wrong only in extending that posture to `allOf`, where a root `type` is the normal, correct shape.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p19","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The repo's hermetic schema-assertion idiom is node:test + node:assert/strict asserting over literal schema objects with no runner config and no mocking framework, as used in the companion metamodel test and the docgen schema test — which is the idiom the new test file extends.","evidence":"Confirmed. metamodel.test.ts:98 => 'assert.equal(validateAgainstSchema(schema, good).ok, true);' and docgen/__tests__/schema.test.ts:22 => 'const validate = ajv.compile(DOCGEN_OUTCOME_SCHEMA);' both resolve verbatim — literal-schema assertions with no runner config. \"from 'node:test'\" and \"from 'node:assert/strict'\" each return result sets capped at 50 with 48-49 in src/, so the convention is repo-wide and the new file extends it rather than introducing anything.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"p20","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"LOW","premise":"The upstream ISSUE-1f7ade1a889013da deliberately left the wrap-versus-reject decision unsettled and imposed two invariants on whichever is chosen: the dead helper must not survive as a false signal of safety, and the constraint must be asserted by a test that does not require a billed live call. The LLD chooses reject (a1), deletes the helper, and adds only hermetic tests — so both invariants are addressed.","evidence":"Confirmed as far as a source probe can reach. The read resolves .insrc/artifacts/ISSUE-1f7ade1a889013da.json, so the upstream artifact exists. Both distinctive phrases — 'false signal of safety' (2 matches) and 'does NOT require a billed live call' (1 match) — are found, with zero src/ hits, which is exactly right for artifact prose: they appear in the ISSUE and in this LLD's trace of it. The cross-artifact trace holds: the ISSUE's two invariants are carried into the LLD, which chooses reject (a1), deletes the helper (ac5), and adds only hermetic tests (ac7).","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"}],"counts":{"high":1,"med":3,"low":17},"reviewedAt":"2026-10-03T18:31:45.014Z","model":"client"},"resolutions":{"p2":{"findingId":"p2","status":"resolved","note":"RESOLVED, but NOT by the option the review recommended. The finding was half right: rootUnionKey DOES return a keyword for CLASSIFY_SCHEMA (src/workflow/triage/classify.ts:84, a root allOf beside a root type:object). The review inferred from that it was OVER-rejection and recommended narrowing the guard to oneOf/anyOf; that narrowing was applied, then WITHDRAWN after a deliberate out-of-band probe refuted it. Both vendors refuse a root allOf even with a root type present — claude: \"input_schema does not support oneOf, allOf, or anyOf at the top level\"; codex: \"In context=(), allOf is not permitted.\" So all three keywords are restored and CLASSIFY_SCHEMA is confirmed genuinely un-sendable to either CLI: the guard reports a real constraint rather than over-rejecting. What the finding DID correctly fix is the inventory miscount, now recorded in backwardCompat with both missed sites named, plus a pinned edge-case row and the verbatim probe evidence in the artifact so this never needs re-probing.","resolvedAt":"2026-10-03T19:02:26.291Z"},"p17":{"findingId":"p17","status":"resolved","note":"RESOLVED by PROBING rather than by scoping down, taking the review’s second option. The external contract is no longer an assumption: five root shapes were sent through both binaries and the verbatim results are recorded in the LLD summary. Confirmed — all three of oneOf/anyOf/allOf are refused at the top level by BOTH vendors; a root type:object does NOT exempt them, which conclusively falsifies normaliseSchemaForAnthropic (injecting a root type clears only the FIRST error and reveals the union error beneath); and a bare root $ref fails on both anyway via the missing-root-type rule. One NEW narrower gap surfaced and is recorded: codex also refuses root enum and root not, which claude’s message does not mention, so the guard covers the three keywords BOTH vendors name and the codex-only pair is a deliberate, documented non-coverage.","resolvedAt":"2026-10-03T19:02:26.294Z"},"p10":{"findingId":"p10","status":"resolved","note":"RESOLVED by removing the unverified number. \"~40 call sites\" is replaced with \"widely used across analyze/, workflow/, mcp/ and both providers\" in both the invariant text and the data-model details, since the probe could only establish a capped 50-match floor. Nothing in the design depended on the figure.","resolvedAt":"2026-10-03T18:34:38.165Z"},"p21":{"findingId":"p21","status":"resolved","note":"ACCEPTED as unverifiable-by-construction. The claim is about the LLD’s own internal migration ordering, which a source-tree probe cannot re-derive; it is self-evident on reading section 7. No artifact change needed.","resolvedAt":"2026-10-03T18:34:38.166Z"}}}}}}
